  "worked_solution": "Let the random variable $X$ be the number of selected cherry creams.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=10$ selected chocolates\n\n- since cherry creams and almond little cakes are in the ratio of $3:2,$ the probability of selecting a cherry cream from the box is $p=\\frac{3}{5}$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(10,\\frac{3}{5}).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x}){(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}{(1-\\frac{3}{5})}^{10-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x}){(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{10-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability of getting at most $9$ cherry creams. This corresponds to $P(X\\le 9).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\le 9)=P(X=0)+P(X=1)+…+P(X=9)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 9) & =1-P(X>9) \\\\  & =1-P(X=10) \\\\  & =1-(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{10}){(\\frac{3}{5})}^{10}{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{10-10} \\\\  & =1-1{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{10}{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{0} \\\\  & =1-0.006\\,047 \\\\  & ≈0.994\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
